<ContentPage title="Governance" subtitle="How we decide together — exploring models for community self-governance.">
  <p>
    BARK's governance model isn't decided yet — and that's deliberate. We believe the community should choose
    how it governs itself. Here are the models we're exploring, and we invite your input.
  </p>

  <h2>Cooperative Model</h2>
  <p>
    Traditional cooperative structure with one-member-one-vote democracy. Legally recognized in Bolivia.
    Proven track record across centuries and cultures.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Legal clarity, institutional recognition, democratic, well-understood</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Can be slow to adapt, geographic constraints on membership, traditional governance overhead</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>DAO Model</h2>
  <p>
    Decentralized Autonomous Organization with on-chain governance. Token-based voting, transparent
    treasury management, smart contract enforcement of decisions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Transparent, enables global participation, programmable rules, resistant to capture</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Legal gray area in Bolivia, technical barrier to participation, plutocratic risk (more tokens = more votes)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hybrid Approach</h2>
  <p>
    Legal cooperative for real-world assets and local operations. DAO layer for community decisions,
    remote participation, and treasury governance. The cooperative owns the land and physical infrastructure;
    the DAO coordinates the community's direction and resource allocation.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Legal protection + digital participation, best of both worlds</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Complexity, potential conflicts between layers, requires careful design</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Other Models We're Studying</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sociocracy</strong> — consent-based decision-making with nested circles of authority</li>
    <li><strong>Ayllu system</strong> — traditional Andean communal governance, deeply relevant to our Bolivian context</li>
    <li><strong>Holacracy</strong> — distributed authority through self-organizing roles</li>
    <li><strong>Liquid democracy</strong> — delegative voting where you can vote directly or delegate your vote to trusted representatives</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Help Us Decide</h2>
  <p>
    Governance design is too important for a small group to decide in isolation. We're collecting perspectives
    from anyone who might participate in or study this community. What works? What fails? What have you
    seen in practice?
  </p>

  <h2>Lessons from the Real World</h2>
  <p>
    Theory is cheap. Here are real governance experiments — some spanning decades, some already failed — and
    what they actually teach about governing a physical community.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Mondragon Corporation</strong> (Basque Country, Spain) — The world's largest cooperative: 70,000+
      employees, over &euro;11 billion in revenue (as of <a href="https://www.mondragon-corporation.com/en/about-us/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2023 annual report</a>). One-member-one-vote governance with elected management, even
      at massive scale. Mondragon proves that cooperatives are not limited to small artisan workshops — they can
      compete with multinational corporations while maintaining democratic governance.
      <a href="https://www.mondragon-corporation.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mondragon-corporation.com</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Auroville</strong> (Tamil Nadu, India) — over 3,000 residents from 60 nations, founded in 1968. A
      cautionary tale: in 2022, the Indian government's Auroville Foundation overrode resident self-governance,
      unilaterally pushing development plans against community consensus. Decades of community building can be
      undermined by external authority in a single administrative action. Lesson: legal structure matters as much
      as internal governance.
      <a href="https://auroville.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">auroville.org</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Zapatista Autonomous Municipalities</strong> (Chiapas, Mexico) — Over 30 years of "mandar obedeciendo"
      (lead by obeying). Rotating leadership, community assemblies, governance structures spanning dozens of autonomous municipalities across Chiapas. In 2023, the Zapatistas restructured their governance due to external pressures — cartel
      violence, youth emigration, economic stress. Shows that even successful governance must adapt continuously;
      no system is set-and-forget.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tamera</strong> (Portugal) — 170 residents using a consent-based "Forum" process for decisions.
      Daily community check-ins where any member can raise issues. Works well at small scale for internal
      cohesion, but raises insularity concerns — the community can become an echo chamber without deliberate
      outside engagement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Ayllu</strong> (Bolivia/Peru) — Indigenous governance directly relevant to BARK's location.
      Based on ayni (reciprocity), minka (communal labor), rotating leadership, and consensus. Constitutionally
      recognized in Bolivia since 2009 under the plurinational state framework. This is not optional historical
      context — it is the living governance reality of our actual neighbors. Any governance model BARK adopts
      must be compatible with and respectful of ayllu principles.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CityDAO</strong> — A DAO that bought 40 acres in Wyoming to test on-chain governance for a physical
      community. The result: token-based voting led to plutocracy (more tokens = more power), governance
      participation hovered at 1&ndash;5%, and the project hit regulatory walls it hadn't anticipated. Lesson:
      on-chain governance alone does not work for physical communities where people share land, water, and
      daily life.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote><p>The pattern is clear: successful long-term communities use consensus-based governance with rotating leadership. Token-based DAO governance alone has not sustained physical communities. Bolivia's ayllu system, tested over centuries, may be a better model than Silicon Valley's latest experiment.</p></blockquote>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Have governance experience or strong opinions?</p>
    <p>We want to hear them. <a href="/en/participate">Share your perspective</a>.</p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
